* t04mode0.a -- CONF68K test t04: I$Open of a file with an access mode of* zero -- no read bit, no write bit, nothing.** Claim (DOCS/claims.md): the manual states outright that a non-directory* file MAY be opened with no bits set, so that a caller can examine its* attributes and size with I$GetStt, and that such a path permits no* actual I/O. This test checks the first half: the open must succeed.* t05 checks the second half on the same kind of path.** Worth stating why this pair exists at all. Mode 0 looks like a caller* mistake, and it usually is one -- POSIX O_RDONLY is 0 while OS-9 read* access is bit 0 set, so C code ported without thought asks for no access* at all. But the manual makes mode 0 legal and useful, so a system that* rejects the open is wrong, and one that then allows a read is also wrong.* Only both halves together match the documented behaviour.*Prgrm set 1Objct set 1ReEnt set $80Type_Lang set (Prgrm<<8)+ObjctAttr_Rev set (ReEnt<<8)+1IOpen set $84ICreate set $83IRead set $89IWrite set $8AISeek set $88IClose set $8FIDelete set $87IGetStt set $8DSSSize set $02ATTRS set $001B*EXPECT set 0* psect t04mode0,Type_Lang,Attr_Rev,0,2048,startstart* Scratch buffer on the stack, never in the code psect: this module is* marked re-entrant and a real system may hold it in ROM, where a write* into its own body would fault rather than just be untidy. sub.l #128,a7 movea.l a7,a4 lea fname(pc),a0 moveq #3,d0 trap #0 dc.w IDelete lea fname(pc),a0 moveq #2,d0 move.w #ATTRS,d1 clr.l d2 trap #0 dc.w ICreate bcs.w setuperr move.w d0,d7 lea data4(pc),a0 moveq #4,d1 moveq #0,d0 move.w d7,d0 trap #0 dc.w IWrite bcs.w setuperr moveq #0,d0 move.w d7,d0 trap #0 dc.w IClose* open with an access mode of zero lea fname(pc),a0 moveq #0,d0 trap #0 dc.w IOpen bcs.s t04err move.w d0,d7 moveq #0,d6 moveq #0,d0 move.w d7,d0 trap #0 dc.w IClose bra.s t04jdgt04err moveq #0,d6 move.w d1,d6t04jdg cmpi.l #EXPECT,d6 beq.s ispass bra.s isfail** --- verdict tail, identical in every test ---* ERROR is not FAIL: it means this test could not establish the situation* it set out to check (its own create/open/write step failed), so it has no* standing to report a verdict on the claim. d6 carries the error code that* stopped it.* setuperr is where every failed create/open/write lands. It falls straight* through into iserror rather than branching to it -- the branch it replaces* was the one far jump whose distance grew with the body, so removing it* removes a per-test range problem instead of solving it twelve times.setuperr moveq #0,d6 move.w d1,d6iserror lea errstr(pc),a5 bra.s reportisfail lea failstr(pc),a5 bra.s reportispass lea passtr(pc),a5report lea idstr(pc),a0 bsr putstr movea.l a5,a0 bsr putstr lea obsstr(pc),a0 bsr putstr move.l d6,d2 bsr put6dec lea expstr(pc),a0 bsr putstr move.l #EXPECT,d2 bsr put6dec lea descstr(pc),a0 bsr putstr bsr putcr bra.w finish* use report.i*fname dc.b "SCRATCH/t04.dat",0data4 dc.b "ABCD"idstr dc.b "RESULT t04 ",0passtr dc.b "PASS",0failstr dc.b "FAIL",0errstr dc.b "ERROR",0obsstr dc.b "  obs=",0expstr dc.b " exp=",0descstr dc.b "  I$Open with no mode bits set succeeds",0 ends